The reason your page might have gone blank is likely due to a naming conflict or trying to extend a component (BigHeading or SubText) that was imported but not yet initialized in that specific way.
To keep it professional, follow the 20px/500 weight suggestion for the sub-description while keeping the main title slightly more prominent for hierarchy.
1. The Styled Components (Add at the TOP)
Paste this right after your import statements, but before the export default function LoginPort() line.
I’ve used the theme object so it automatically picks up your application's colors instead of hard-coding them.
import { styled } from "@mui/material/styles";
import { Typography, Box } from "@mui/material";

// Wrapper to manage the vertical spacing of the long text block
const HeroHeaderBox = styled(Box)(({ theme }) => ({
  marginBottom: theme.spacing(4),
  maxWidth: "600px", // Limits width so long text doesn't stretch across the whole screen
}));

const WelcomeTitle = styled(Typography)(({ theme }) => ({
  fontSize: "1.8rem", // Slightly larger than subtext for hierarchy
  fontWeight: 700,
  lineHeight: 1.3,
  marginBottom: theme.spacing(2),
  color: "inherit", // Uses the parent text color (likely white or primary contrast)
  letterSpacing: "-0.01em",
}));

const WelcomeSubText = styled(Typography)(({ theme }) => ({
  fontSize: "20px",    // As per your suggestion
  fontWeight: 500,     // As per your suggestion
  lineHeight: 1.6,     // Increased line-height makes long paragraphs much easier to read
  color: "inherit", 
  opacity: 0.9,        // Softens the long text to look more professional
  textAlign: "justify", // Makes long blocks of text look clean on the edges
}));

2. The JSX Update (Where to add what)
Locate the LeftCol section inside your LoginPort return statement. Replace the existing title and subtext lines with this:
{/* LEFT COLUMN */}
<LeftCol>
  <HeroHeaderBox>
    <WelcomeTitle variant="h1">
      {content?.hero?.welcomeTitle}
    </WelcomeTitle>
    
    <WelcomeSubText>
      {content?.hero?.subText}
    </WelcomeSubText>
  </HeroHeaderBox>

  {/* Keep your Legal Warning section as it is below */}
  <BigHeading>{content?.legal?.title}</BigHeading>
  <FeatureList>
    {content?.legal?.items.map((item, idx) => (
      <FeatureItem key={idx}>
        <Bullet /> {item}
      </FeatureItem>
    ))}
  </FeatureList>
</LeftCol>

Why this is better:
 * Readability: Setting a maxWidth of 600px is a standard "Senior Dev" move. Humans find it hard to read sentences that are too wide; keeping it constrained makes the login page feel balanced.
 * No Hard-Coding: By using theme.spacing and inherit colors, your design will stay consistent even if you change your app's primary colors later.
 * Line Height: For a 20px font, a 1.6 line height prevents the "wall of text" feeling.
